  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 403 AM EDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nModels show a summer-like weather pattern to begin the long term \nwith the Atlantic high ridge axis remaining just north of the area \nand keeping a generally E/ESE wind regime in place through the \nweekend.  \n\nEarly afternoon convection will likely initiate along the east coast \nsea breeze, then gradually pushing inland and west through the late \nafternoon and early evening hours each day. Models remain fairly \nconsistent in showing a rather sharp gradient of POPs with 30-40% \nfor the Atlantic metro areas, and upwards of 70% for the Gulf coast. \n\nFor next week, a return to a more widespread wet and unsettled \npattern is shown by guidance as a trough deepens over the E \nCONUS. An associated sfc boundary reaches the northern portions of\nthe peninsula in the Monday-Tuesday timeframe, although the \nspecific timing of this potential front remains uncertain. But \nregardless of the final outcome, guidance in general show coverage\nbecoming widespread by late Monday and through Tuesday, with \nhighest NBM POPs well into the 80% range by Tuesday afternoon. \n\nTemperatures seems to steadily warm up back into the upper 80s to \nlow 90s through the weekend, especially as easterly flow and \nmoisture return to the area.",
